A = {
"na179125": dict(
 bluf="The Bill of Rights wrote individual liberties into the Constitution as limits on federal power, the price of winning over those who feared a strong central government. Through court rulings, especially after the 14th Amendment, it became the core of American civil liberties.",
 why="Supporters of the Constitution promised amendments to win over Anti-Federalists who feared central power.",
 then="At first it limited only the federal government; after the Civil War the 14th Amendment let courts apply it to the states, mostly during the 20th century.",
 now="Free speech, gun rights and police searches are still argued over in Bill of Rights terms.",
 lens="Rights on paper become real only when courts and citizens enforce them."),
"d-varennes": dict(
 bluf="The royal family's failed escape destroyed public trust in Louis XVI and made a republic thinkable. It turned the king from a symbol of national unity into a suspected traitor.",
 lens="A ruler caught trying to flee loses the claim to lead; legitimacy can collapse in a single night."),
"as179325": dict(
 bluf="Qianlong's rejection of the Macartney mission reflected China's confidence in a tribute system with itself at the centre. Britain, wanting trade on equal terms, turned to opium and then to force to open China.",
 why="China saw itself as the centre of civilization, receiving foreigners as tribute-bearers; Britain wanted diplomatic equality, more open ports and a resident ambassador.",
 then="Britain's trade deficit from buying tea kept growing, and it turned to selling Indian opium to China, which led to the Opium Wars.",
 now="It is often taught as China's missed chance to engage the industrial age, though historians now argue that view exaggerates Qing ignorance of the outside world.",
 lens="Clashes between incompatible ideas of international order, here tribute versus treaty, rarely end in compromise."),
"na179326": dict(
 bluf="The cotton gin made short-staple cotton profitable across the Deep South, reviving and expanding slavery at a time when it might have declined. Cotton became America's leading export, tying Southern slavery to Britain's textile mills.",
 lens="Technology that makes a coercive system more profitable can entrench it rather than make it obsolete."),
"eu179358": dict(
 bluf="Executing the king made the Revolution irreversible and turned it into a war against Europe's monarchies. It marked France's definitive break with divine-right monarchy.",
 lens="Killing a king closes the door to compromise for both sides."),
"eu179358b": dict(
 bluf="The Terror showed how a revolution claiming to defend liberty could become government by fear, justified by war and internal enemies. It became the archetype of revolutionary violence, cited by critics of revolution ever since.",
 now="'Terror' entered politics as a word for government by fear, and conservative thought from Edmund Burke onward drew on it as a warning against revolutionary utopias.",
 lens="Emergencies justify extraordinary powers; watch whether those powers end when the emergency does."),
"d-thermidor": dict(
 bluf="Robespierre's fall ended the Terror and began the Revolution's retreat from radicalism. 'Thermidor' became the name for the moment a revolution turns back toward order.",
 lens="Revolutionaries who rule by fear eventually unite their colleagues against them."),
"af179513": dict(
 bluf="Britain's seizure of the Cape put a key station on the sea route to India under British control and brought Dutch-speaking settlers under British rule. British policies, including the abolition of slavery, later drove the Boers inland, setting up a century of conflict that shaped South Africa.",
 why="During the wars with revolutionary France, which had overrun the Netherlands, Britain wanted to protect its route to India.",
 lens="Strategic bases taken for one reason become colonies with politics of their own."),
"me-napoleonegypt": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon's invasion was the first major European military intervention in the Middle East since the Crusades, and it exposed Ottoman weakness. It shattered Mamluk power, opening the way for Muhammad Ali's modernizing state, and launched European scholarly and imperial interest in the region.",
 now="Modern Egyptology began with the expedition's 'Description de l'Égypte'. Edward Said's 'Orientalism' treats the expedition as the start of a European tradition of studying the East in order to dominate it.",
 lens="A failed invasion can still transform a region by destroying its old elite."),
"eu179959": dict(
 bluf="The coup of 18 Brumaire ended the Revolution's experiments with parliamentary government and began Napoleon's personal rule. He kept the Revolution's civil reforms while ending its political freedom, a template for modern authoritarianism with popular backing.",
 lens="When a revolutionary government is weak and at war, a popular general is often the one who takes over."),
"sa-tipu": dict(
 bluf="Tipu Sultan's death removed the East India Company's most determined Indian enemy and secured southern India for Britain. It showed that even modernizing Indian states could not match the Company's resources.",
 now="Tipu is a divisive figure in India today: an anti-colonial hero to some, a persecutor of Hindus and Christians to others. Mysore's iron-cased rockets inspired Britain's Congreve rockets.",
 lens="Alliances with distant powers rarely save a local ruler; they often give the enemy a reason to strike first."),
"d-toussaint": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon's capture of Toussaint aimed to decapitate the Haitian Revolution and restore slavery. It backfired: news that France had brought back slavery elsewhere drove Haitians to fight on to full independence.",
 lens="Removing a leader rarely ends a movement whose followers fear what defeat would mean."),
"na180327": dict(
 bluf="The Louisiana Purchase doubled the size of the United States and gave it the Mississippi and New Orleans, making it a continental power. It also reopened the question of slavery in new territories, which escalated over the next half-century into civil war.",
 why="Napoleon needed money for war with Britain and had given up on an American empire after his army was destroyed in Haiti. Jefferson wanted New Orleans, through which western farmers shipped their crops.",
 then="It opened the West to settlement and set off decades of fights over whether new states would allow slavery. The Native nations who lived there were not consulted, and their lands were taken over the following century.",
 now="Fifteen of today's states lie partly or wholly in the territory.",
 lens="Distant powers sell territory they cannot defend; the buyer gains land, and new problems."),
"d-marbury": dict(
 bluf="Marbury v. Madison established judicial review: the Supreme Court's power to strike down laws that conflict with the Constitution. It made the courts a co-equal branch and the final interpreter of the Constitution.",
 lens="Institutions sometimes gain power by appearing to give it up: Marshall lost a small case to claim a large principle."),
"d-vertieres": dict(
 bluf="Vertières ended Napoleon's attempt to retake Saint-Domingue and restore slavery, leading directly to Haitian independence. Yellow fever and Haitian resistance destroyed one of the largest expeditionary forces France ever sent overseas.",
 lens="Disease and local knowledge can defeat even the strongest expeditionary armies."),
"af180414": dict(
 bluf="Usman dan Fodio's jihad built the Sokoto Caliphate, one of Africa's largest 19th-century states, uniting Hausa lands under Fulani-led Islamic rule. Its institutions survived British conquest and still shape northern Nigeria's politics and religious identity.",
 why="Protest against corrupt Hausa rulers, heavy taxes and what reformers saw as impure Islamic practice; the grievances of Fulani herders fed the movement.",
 then="The caliphate ruled northern Nigeria until British conquest in 1903. Britain then governed through its emirs ('indirect rule'), preserving the old elite.",
 now="The Sultan of Sokoto remains Nigeria's most senior Muslim leader, and Nigeria's north-south divide partly reflects the caliphate's legacy. Boko Haram claims to revive its jihad, though Sokoto's establishment rejects it.",
 lens="Religious reform movements can become state-builders, and their institutions often outlast later conquerors."),
"eu180460": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon's self-coronation turned the Revolution's republic into a hereditary empire while keeping its legal reforms. It signalled power based on merit and force rather than Church or tradition.",
 lens="Strongmen often keep a revolution's institutions while discarding its political freedoms."),
"la180413": dict(
 bluf="Haiti's independence created the first Black republic and the first state to abolish slavery permanently. The great powers answered with isolation, and France with a ruinous indemnity, showing how threatening a free Black state was to the slaveholding world.",
 then="Dessalines ordered the killing of most of the remaining French whites in 1804. France forced Haiti to pay a crushing 'indemnity' for its freedom, which Haiti paid off for over a century.",
 lens="New states that challenge the existing order are often punished economically, not only militarily."),
"na180428": dict(
 bluf="Lewis and Clark mapped the new Louisiana territory and a route to the Pacific, strengthening US claims to the Oregon Country. The expedition previewed how American expansion would depend on Native knowledge and lead to Native dispossession.",
 lens="Exploration is a claim: mapping a territory is the first step to controlling it."),
"d-civilcode": dict(
 bluf="The Napoleonic Code gave France one uniform civil law based on equality before the law and secure property, ending feudal privilege. Spread by conquest and imitation, it became the basis of civil law in much of Europe, Latin America and beyond, though it also entrenched male authority over women.",
 lens="Legal codes outlast the empires that impose them."),
"me-muhammadali": dict(
 bluf="Muhammad Ali built the Middle East's first modernizing state, with a conscript army, state factories and European-trained officials. His Egypt nearly overthrew the Ottomans until European powers intervened, showing both the promise and the limits of defensive modernization.",
 lens="Copying Europe's armies to resist Europe can work, but great powers intervene when a local power grows too strong."),
"eu180561": dict(
 bluf="Trafalgar destroyed the French and Spanish fleets and secured British command of the sea for a century. It ended Napoleon's hopes of invading Britain and made economic blockade his main weapon against it.",
 lens="Naval dominance lets an island power survive its allies' defeats on land and outlast continental empires."),
"eu180561b": dict(
 bluf="Austerlitz destroyed the Third Coalition and made Napoleon master of Central Europe. It led directly to the end of the Holy Roman Empire and the reorganization of Germany.",
 lens="Decisive victories can redraw political maps faster than diplomacy."),
"eu180662": dict(
 bluf="The end of the Holy Roman Empire removed the thousand-year-old framework of German politics. Napoleon's consolidation of hundreds of German states into a few dozen paved the way for German nationalism and eventual unification under Prussia.",
 now="Voltaire's quip that it was 'neither holy, nor Roman, nor an empire' captures how hollow it had become. Historians sometimes compare the EU to it as a loose, many-layered union.",
 lens="Old institutions can survive long after losing real power, until a shock sweeps them away."),
"eu180763": dict(
 bluf="Britain, then the largest slave-trading nation, banned the Atlantic trade and used its navy and diplomacy to suppress other countries' trade. It was a turning point in the global end of slavery, though slavery itself continued for decades in the Americas.",
 lens="When the world's dominant power changes its position on a practice, it can enforce that change globally."),
"la180814": dict(
 bluf="The Portuguese court's flight made Rio de Janeiro the capital of a European empire and turned Brazil from colony into kingdom. It explains why Brazil became independent as one unified monarchy rather than splintering like Spanish America.",
 then="Brazil gained schools, banks and a printing press. When King João VI returned to Portugal in 1821, his son Pedro stayed and declared Brazilian independence in 1822.",
 lens="Where authority sits during a crisis shapes what comes after: Brazil stayed united because it already had a court."),
"na180829": dict(
 bluf="The United States banned the importing of enslaved people at the first moment the Constitution allowed, but slavery itself kept growing. The internal slave trade from the Upper South to the cotton states became a huge business that tore families apart.",
 lens="Banning a trade without banning the practice can make the domestic trade more profitable."),
"jp-phaeton": dict(
 bluf="The Phaeton incident showed that Japan's coastal defences could not stop a single Western warship. It hardened shogunate policy against foreign ships and foreshadowed the confrontation with Perry in 1853.",
 lens="Isolation policies tend to harden after humiliations rather than adapt."),
"la181015": dict(
 bluf="Hidalgo's call to revolt began Mexico's war of independence as a mass uprising of the Indigenous and mestizo poor. Its violence frightened Creole elites, which is why independence, when it came in 1821, was led by conservatives.",
 why="Napoleon's overthrow of Spain's king created a crisis of authority, on top of Creole resentment of Spanish-born officials and Indigenous and mestizo grievances over land and tribute.",
 then="The war lasted 11 years. Independence was finally won in 1821 by the conservative officer Agustín de Iturbide, who briefly made himself emperor.",
 lens="Revolutions begun from below are often finished by elites who fear them."),
"oc-kamehameha": dict(
 bluf="Kamehameha used Western guns and advisers to unify the Hawaiian Islands into one kingdom. The kingdom won international recognition but was later overthrown by American settlers and annexed by the United States.",
 lens="Local rulers who adopt foreign technology can build strong states, but they also draw in foreign interests."),
"d-luddites": dict(
 bluf="The Luddites were skilled workers resisting machines that cut their wages and status, not simply opponents of technology. The state's harsh repression showed that industrialization's costs would fall on workers who had no political voice.",
 lens="Technological change creates losers, and how a society treats them shapes its politics for generations."),
"eu181264": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon's invasion of Russia destroyed his Grande Armée and broke his aura of invincibility. It triggered the coalition that toppled him and made Russia the dominant land power in Europe for the next generation.",
 story="In June 1812 Napoleon crossed into Russia with about 600,000 men. The Russians retreated, burning crops and villages. After the bloody battle of Borodino he took Moscow in September, but the city burned and the tsar refused to negotiate. The winter retreat, harried by Cossacks, destroyed the army; fewer than 100,000 came out.",
 why="Russia had broken Napoleon's Continental System, his blockade of British trade, and Napoleon wanted to force the tsar back into line.",
 then="Prussia and Austria joined Russia and Britain, and Napoleon was beaten at Leipzig (1813) and exiled in 1814.",
 now="Russia's strategy of trading space for time, and the role of winter, became part of its national memory, and Hitler's 1941 invasion repeated Napoleon's mistakes.",
 lens="Overextended invasions fail on logistics: the deeper an army goes, the weaker its supply lines."),
"na181230": dict(
 bluf="The War of 1812 ended in stalemate between the United States and Britain, but it confirmed American independence, boosted nationalism and broke Native resistance in the Old Northwest. For Canada, repelling American invasions became part of its founding identity.",
 lens="Wars that end in a draw can still have big losers: here, the Native nations."),
"d-borodino": dict(
 bluf="Borodino was the bloodiest day of the Napoleonic Wars, and it opened the road to Moscow without destroying the Russian army. Napoleon won the field but not the war.",
 lens="Taking an enemy's capital means little if its army and government survive."),
"d-leipzig": dict(
 bluf="Leipzig, the 'Battle of the Nations', drove Napoleon out of Germany and set up the allied invasion of France. It became a founding myth of German national awakening.",
 lens="Coalitions defeat a would-be hegemon once enough powers decide that staying out is more dangerous than joining."),
"d-elba": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon's first abdication ended his empire and restored the Bourbon monarchy. Exiling him so close to France proved a mistake: he escaped within a year.",
 lens="Half-measures with a defeated leader can invite a comeback."),
"eu181565": dict(
 bluf="Waterloo ended Napoleon's return and 23 years of war between France and the rest of Europe. It confirmed the Vienna settlement and opened a century without a general European war.",
 lens="Coalitions win when they hold together: Wellington's stand worked because Blücher kept his word."),
"eu181565b": dict(
 bluf="The Congress of Vienna redrew Europe after Napoleon on the principle of balance among the great powers and created the 'Concert of Europe' to manage crises together. It prevented a general European war for a century, but by suppressing nationalism and liberal revolutions it stored up conflicts for later.",
 why="To restore order and stop any single power from dominating Europe again.",
 then="Conservative powers cooperated to crush liberal and nationalist revolts in the 1820s. The settlement eroded with the revolutions of 1848 and the unification of Italy and Germany, but no general war broke out until 1914.",
 now="It is still studied as a model of how rivals can build a lasting peace; Henry Kissinger's study of it shaped his Cold War diplomacy.",
 lens="Durable peace settlements bring the defeated power back in: France was included in 1815, unlike Germany in 1919."),
"la181616": dict(
 bluf="Argentina's declaration completed the break with Spain in the Río de la Plata and launched San Martín's campaigns that freed Chile and Peru. Decades of civil war between Buenos Aires and the provinces followed, shaping Argentina's lasting divide between port and interior.",
 lens="Independence settles who rules from outside; who rules inside is fought over afterward."),
"d-ricardo": dict(
 bluf="Ricardo's theory of comparative advantage showed that even a country better at producing everything gains by specializing and trading. It became the core intellectual case for free trade.",
 lens="The gains from trade depend on relative, not absolute, efficiency."),
}
